WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:05.200
Okay, in this section, we're going to do a quick review of calibration.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.550
So, we've already covered what calibration is and why calibration is required.

3
00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.550
But again, to reiterate, as we continue our calibration process,

4
00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:18.550
calibration is required in order to create accurate compositions from CG renders and live video in Unreal Engine,

5
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:25.550
as a virtual camera needs to accurately simulate the physical camera used to capture the real-world video footage.

6
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:31.550
Now, the virtual camera position and orientation must closely match the position and orientation of the physical camera,

7
00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:37.549
and the physical tracking information for that physical camera must match the exact timing of the video feed

8
00:00:37.549 --> 00:00:43.549
to ensure that each video frame is accurately synced to the position and orientation of the camera at any given time.

9
00:00:43.549 --> 00:00:49.549
When you complete this correctly, it is possible to evaluate your calibrated lens file for any focus and zoom position

10
00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:52.549
by interpolating between the stored focus and zoom points.

11
00:00:52.549 --> 00:00:56.549
And as we move through our tracking and calibration sections, we're going to be using this setup.

12
00:00:56.549 --> 00:01:02.549
Each major component is required, but your solutions may differ than what we have on the Latte stage.

13
00:01:02.549 --> 00:01:06.549
Now, one thing I want to note is that if you don't do lens distortion mapping before this,

14
00:01:06.549 --> 00:01:11.549
you will run into the issue where the information you pass along for lens distortion and nodal point calibration

15
00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:15.549
will not be accurate for your post team.

16
00:01:15.549 --> 00:01:23.840
So, set extensions and anything that you need to add to your shots is going to be requiring you to do this calibration step.

17
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.480
Now, as you get started, there are some plugins that need to be enabled.

18
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:33.480
Again, camera calibration, lens distortion, which is depreciated, and then the OpenCV lens distortion.

19
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:37.480
Now, we move to OpenCV lens distortion instead of using our lens distortion plugin,

20
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.480
so that the OpenCV system is actually going to take on the bulk of that.

21
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.480
This was a change that was made in 5.0 as OpenCV takes over that function.

22
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.480
And again, as we move into more camera calibration, you'll see how that is important.

23
00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:57.090
Now, there is one element that we built in our last class that you need to make sure is still active,

24
00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.090
and that is our media profile.

25
00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:02.090
If you have not already built your media profile, please refer to the other section.

26
00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:08.090
The media profile is required as an asset that your lens file is going to utilize in order to locate your video source,

27
00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:15.090
and that is used by Composer to overlay your CG elements in Unreal Engine inside the lens file asset editor for calibration.

28
00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:21.090
Now, the media profile proxy configuration will automatically detect the number of sources available in your configuration,

29
00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.090
but you may need to manually set that depending on what your setup is like.

30
00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:30.090
Also, you are going to need additional lens files, as we've already built those,

31
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.090
if you have multiple cameras and lens configurations.

32
00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:38.090
If you have a single camera body and multiple lenses, you will need a lens file for each.

33
00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:41.180
Okay, so that finishes up this section.

34
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:45.180
And as we get ready, we're going to move on and talk about some other dependencies for calibration.
